\cf3 Mortgage lenders use the 10-year Treasury yield as a benchmark for long-term, low-risk returns. Since mortgages are long-term loans, lenders price them by adding a spread (for risk, overhead, and profit) to this benchmark. Thus, as the 10-year Treasury yield moves, the base cost of borrowing for lenders changes, directly influencing the rates offered to homebuyers. It reflects the market's expectation for long-term interest rates. (Question: Why do 10 year Treasury bond yields affect mortgage interest rates?)\cell \row

