\cf3 Inflation primarily occurs when the money supply grows faster than the economy's ability to produce goods and services. This means more money is chasing relatively fewer items, driving up prices. Key mechanisms include: 1. **Demand-Pull:** Strong consumer demand outstrips supply. 2. **Cost-Push:** Rising production costs (e.g., wages, materials) are passed to consumers. 3. **Monetary Expansion:** Central banks increasing the money supply dilutes its purchasing power. Essentially, each unit of currency buys less, diminishing its value over time. (Question: Can you explain the mechanism of money inflation?)\cell \row

