\cf3 Direct funds have lower expense ratios than regular funds, as they don't include distributor commissions. This expense ratio difference directly causes the return disparity. The *inconsistency* in this return difference often stems from the underlying fund's varying performance across different periods. While the percentage difference in expense ratios between direct and regular plans for the same fund is relatively stable, its absolute impact on returns will fluctuate with the fund's overall gains or losses, making the observed gap appear inconsistent. (Question: Why is there inconsistent returns difference between direct and regular Mutual Funds?)\cell \row

